Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the \u201cability to consummate\u201d concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: \u201cMaterial Adverse Effect\u201d means any change, event, effect, or occurrence that, individually or in the aggregate, (a) has a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (b) impairs the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that in the case of clause (a), none of the following shall be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect: (i) changes in the economy or financial markets in general; (ii) changes in laws or regulations after the date of this Agreement; (iii) acts of war or terrorism; (iv) natural disasters; (v) any change in the Company's stock price or trading volume; (vi) any failure by the Company to meet internal or external projections or forecasts; (vii) any action taken with the written consent of Parent; except that the effects of clauses (i) through (iv) shall be considered in determining whether a Material Adverse Effect has occurred to the extent that such effects disproportionately impact the Company compared to other companies in the same industry. Furthermore, for the purpose of clause (b), the ability to consummate the transaction is not affected by the aforementioned carveouts unless specifically stated otherwise in this Agreement. (Pages 12-13)\nAnswer:
